text:
maya: Heads-up — hit a weird combo in the JS/TS onboarding sample for edge workers (Cloudflare/Netlify edge). Types blow up when project is ESM + `type: \"module\"` in package.json and tsconfig set to ESNext.

sam: ugh, pattern seen before. What error do you get?

maya: `No overload matches this call` on our `client.chat.completions.create(...)` wrapper. The signatures have generics and overloads; TS seems to pick the wrong overload and then treats the request body as `unknown`. Minimal repro: 
```ts
// handler.ts (edge)
import { Redwood } from 'redwood-inference';
export default async function handler(req: Request) {
  const r = new Redwood({ apiKey: Deno.env.get('REDWOOD_KEY') });
  const resp = await r.chat.completions.create({
    model: 'gpt-open-1',
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'hi' }],
  });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(resp));
}
```
TS error: `Argument of type '{ model: string; messages: { role: string; content: string; }[]; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'CreateParams'.
  Overload 1 of 3, 'create(params: CreateParams): Promise<...>', gave the following error...`

igor: does it happen only in edge build or also locally with `tsc`?

maya: locally with `tsc --build` it compiles, but when bundled for the edge (esbuild + target es2020) or when running in Node ESM mode I get the type error in editors (vscode/tsserver). So it's a tooling/tsconfig mismatch rather than runtime.

lee: my guess: conditional exports + types package entrypoints. We recently added separate `.d.ts` paths for `./edge` vs `./node` and some editors pick the wrong entry. package.json `exports` + `types` fields can cause TS to resolve declarations differently in ESM vs CJS.

maya: yeah, looked at `package.json` — we have `"type": "module"` and exports like: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": { "import": "dist/index.mjs", "require": "dist/index.cjs" },
  "./edge": { "import": "dist/edge.mjs" }
},
"types": "dist/index.d.ts",
"typesVersions": { "*": { "*": [ "dist/*" ] } }
```
But TS still resolves `index.d.ts` which has overloads that assume Node runtime (Buffer, fs types) and those overloads are slightly different (generics using `any` vs `unknown`). Editor ends up choosing the wrong overload.

eva: two things to try: 1) ensure `types` points at a top-level entry that re-exports conditional types with `/// <reference types=\"edge\" />` style or a separate `index.edge.d.ts`; 2) make overloads more robust by replacing `any` with `unknown` and narrowing inside implementation. Also add `moduleResolution: 'bundler'` for ESM projects — it helps editors with conditional exports.

sam: also check `skipLibCheck` — if our d.ts references `node` types but the project doesn't include `@types/node`, editors will complain. Quick workaround: add `types: ['@types/node']` in tsconfig or set `skipLibCheck: true` in sample app tsconfig to unblock onboarding.

maya: good ideas. Another quirk: our SDK uses overloads like `create<T>(params: CreateParams<T>): Promise<Result<T>>` plus several non-generic `create(params: OtherParams)` overloads. TS will sometimes prefer a non-generic overload when `module: 'ESNext'` + `isolatedModules` is true. The fix I found locally: flatten the overloads into a single generic with optional fields — it's less ergonomic but much more stable across editor/tsc/bundler combos.

lee: can you paste the exact minimal type signatures causing trouble?

maya: simplified: 
```ts
// problematic.d.ts
export interface CreateParams<T = any> { input: T }
export function create<T>(params: CreateParams<T>): Promise<unknown>;
export function create(params: { input: string }): Promise<string>;
```
Editor thinks my call matches 2nd overload and then complains about generics. If I rewrite as: 
```ts
export function create<T = unknown>(params: CreateParams<T> | { input: string }): Promise<unknown>;
```
it becomes stable.

igor: that tracks. Overloads + inference across ESM/CJS declaration resolution is brittle. For DX we should favor one stable surface for types (single signature) and keep ergonomic overloads behind a separate `@types/compat` package or similar.

devex-bot: Reminder: repo policy prefers minimal surface area in top-level d.ts to avoid cross-tooling resolution issues. See docs: #typing-guidelines.

maya: Plan: I'll open a small PR with two changes: 1) make top-level declarations a single stable signature that covers generic + string literal fast path; 2) add a devex onboarding tsconfig to samples that sets `moduleResolution: 'bundler'` and `skipLibCheck: true` with comments. I'll also add a note in the edge sample about `type: \"module\"` and conditional exports.

sam: :thumbsup: please link the sample app commit — we should also update the migration guide for customers using CJS -> ESM.

eva: I'll pair-review. Also can we add an automated test that runs `tsc --noEmit` against the sample apps matrix (node esm, node cjs, edge bundler) in CI? Catches these before release.

maya: yes, adding that to CI. Thanks team — this explains a bunch of onboarding DMs we've seen.

lee: fwiw, we should track this under DX-typing in the backlog — these are high friction for new users.

maya: filed RH-2123 (DX-typing) with repro and proposed fixes. I'll post PR link here when ready. :rocket:
